] [17592186397219 #edb/ref 1080 "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397219 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397219 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e417753c-3dcf-5b7e-8c3e-55d245a9b3a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397220 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pm 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397220 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397220 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9ff25083-915f-5352-b85b-65678f3833df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397221 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pm \\frac{3}{14}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1eb5523-b928-553e-8031-34e6f2d343f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397222 #edb/ref 1080 "$-\\frac{3}{14}$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397222 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397222 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "182b3678-ac7b-5812-986d-8764905ed502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186397258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397252 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-20\\lambda {y}^{′}+100{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e67017cf-eb62-5988-9350-d0872682f901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397252 #edb/ref 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda x}+B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 10A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{20\\lambda }+2B{e}^{20\\lambda }+2(10A\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }+B{e}^{20\\lambda }+20B\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(10A\\lambda +B+20B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+20A\\lambda +2B+40B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+(4+40\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda  & 1+10\\lambda  \\\\ 1+20\\lambda  & 4(1+10\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+10\\lambda )-(1+10\\lambda )(1+20\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -(1+20\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -1-20\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )(20\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186397253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186397253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186397255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175